/***********************************************************************************************************************
da4_searchhandlersource.probesql -- dbo.uspSearchHandlerSource (script 506).

Run: sqlcmd -S localhost -E -C -I -d RCRAInfo -y 0 -i "C:\~MDE-55TT2J4\RCRAInfo\build\tmp\da4_searchhandlersource.probesql"
-I is REQUIRED. dbo.HandlerSource carries a filtered unique index (UX_dbo_HandlerSource_Natural), and any write to a
table with one needs SET QUOTED_IDENTIFIER ON or the insert fails with error 1934 naming indexed views.

WHAT THIS PROBE IS FOR. 506 is the last read of Workstream DA and the only procedure in this database that takes a
free-text parameter. Four earlier headers -- 501, 503, 504 and 505 -- record a rule in advance for exactly this moment:
the search term is not logged. This file's job is to turn as much of 506's header as possible into something a run can
fail on, and in particular sections W (the LIKE escaping, where a typed % would otherwise match every handler in
Maryland), R (the seven MatchRank levels and the two mutually exclusive branches) and L (the log rows).

CONVENTIONS THIS FILE FOLLOWS, ALL OF THEM LOAD-BEARING:

  * TWO fixture prefixes, not one, and the second one exists because of 506's own branch rule. MDPROBE0506% is not
    identifier-shaped -- its third character is a letter -- so a fixture built only on that prefix could never exercise
    the identifier branch at all. MD9990506% is MD followed by digits, which is the shape a real handler ID has, and it
    is unmistakably a probe value. The reset and the cleanup cover BOTH.
  * SOFT DELETE ONLY. There is no hard delete anywhere in this database, so cleanup sets IsDeleted = 1 and the fixture
    rows stay. The reset at the top retires anything a previous run left visible, which is what makes this file
    re-runnable.
  * REFUSALS use plain EXEC and successes use INSERT ... EXEC. That is not stylistic: INSERT ... EXEC opens a
    transaction, and a procedure that throws inside one leaves the transaction doomed (error 3930), so the CATCH cannot
    write its log row and the section L assertions would measure nothing.
  * INSERT ... EXEC IS ITSELF AN ASSERTION. #Search names all 21 columns in the procedure's order, so a change to the
    projection's list, order or types fails the insert rather than passing silently. It also succeeds on an EMPTY result
    set and fails on a MISSING one, which a row count cannot tell apart -- and that distinction is the whole point of
    every zero-row case in section W.
  * HandlerSourceId IS AN IDENTITY, SO THE IDS ARE CAPTURED, NOT LOOKED UP. #Ids holds one row per fixture row, each
    taken from SCOPE_IDENTITY () immediately after its INSERT. Looking them up afterwards by natural key is the obvious
    alternative and it is wrong: soft-deleted rows from earlier runs share the key. Temp tables survive across GO on one
    connection; local variables do not.
  * ORDER is asserted with STRING_AGG over Ordinal, against a hand-computed expected string. The expectations are
    derived from the fixture table below by hand rather than by re-running 506's own predicate, so a wrong answer cannot
    agree with itself. Fixture names begin with distinct letters (A, B, C, D, E, P, Z) precisely so that the name
    tiebreaker's expected order cannot turn on a collation subtlety.
  * THE LOG ASSERTIONS ARE RELATIVE TO A BASELINE. logs.ExecutionLog is append-only and cleanup does not touch it, so a
    second run of this file would double any absolute count. #Base holds the highest ExecutionLogId that existed before
    section A, and every count in section L is taken above it.
  * PRINT NEVER CONTAINS A SUBQUERY. PRINT CONCAT (..., (SELECT ...)) is error 1046, and it is a COMPILE error, so it
    takes its whole batch down with it -- including the CREATE TABLE above it. Every count is hoisted into a variable
    first.

WHAT THE FIXTURE IS. Eleven rows: eight visible, three that must never appear.

  Label  HandlerId      Cur   Del  HandlerName               SiteLocationCity   What it is for
  X3     MDPROBE05063   1     0    EPSILON PLAIN CO          Rockville          matched only by an id substring
  X4     MDPROBE05064   1     0    ZZQUARTZ PLATING CO       Baltimore          MatchRank 4, name prefix
  X5     MDPROBE05065   1     0    BETA ZZQUARTZ WORKS       Frederick          MatchRank 5, name contains
  X6     MDPROBE05066   1     0    CITY MATCH ONLY CO        ZZQUARTZTOWN       MatchRank 6, city prefix
  X7     MDPROBE05067   1     0    DOWNTOWN MATCH CO         WEST ZZQUARTZ      MatchRank 7, city contains
  X8     MDPROBE05068   1     0    AB1SOLVENTS INC           Cumberland         the id-shaped-term COST, section R7
  I1     MD9990506001   1     0    PLAIN ID ONE              Annapolis          MatchRank 1, exact id
  I2     MD9990506002   1     0    PLAIN ID TWO              Salisbury          MatchRank 2, id prefix
  E1     MDPROBE05069   1     1    ZZQUARTZ HIDDEN DELETED   Baltimore          soft-deleted
  E2     MDPROBE0506A   0     0    ZZQUARTZ HIDDEN OLD       Baltimore          superseded version
  E3     MDPROBE0506B   NULL  0    ZZQUARTZ HIDDEN NULLCUR   Baltimore          never stamped by the reconcile pass

THE THREE HIDDEN ROWS ALL CARRY ZZQUARTZ IN THE NAME ON PURPOSE. They are the three separate ways dbo.vwHandlerSource
excludes a row, and each is a row the search WOULD find if 506 read the base table or restated the filter wrongly. E3 in
particular: `CurrentRecord = 1` excludes NULL, which is easy to get wrong when reading the view.

THE TWO SEARCH TERMS THE WHOLE FILE TURNS ON:

  N'ZZQUARTZ'  -- text branch. Matches X4, X5, X6, X7 and nothing else, at MatchRank 4, 5, 6 and 7 respectively. One
                  term, four ranks, one row each, so the rank assertion cannot pass by accident.
  N'ROBE0506'  -- text branch, and it is a substring of every MDPROBE0506% id. Matches X3 to X8 -- six rows, all at
                  MatchRank 3 -- and NOT I1 or I2, whose ids are MD999.... Six rows at one rank is what makes the name
                  tiebreaker and the paging assertions meaningful.

Neither term is identifier-shaped: ZZQUARTZ's third character is Q and ROBE0506's is B, and 506 requires a digit there.
That is deliberate -- it means both terms exercise the three-column contains-scan.

WHAT THIS PROBE CANNOT ASSERT, STATED HERE BECAUSE EACH ONE MATTERS:

  1. THE @Take UPPER CLAMP. @Take is clamped to 1..100 and the fixture's widest result is six rows, so a call asking for
     1000 cannot be told from a call asking for 100. The lower clamp IS observable (P4, P5) and the upper one is
     asserted by reading the file. Building a 101-row fixture to prove it would mean 101 rows of real-looking handler
     data in dbo.HandlerSource, permanently, because there is no hard delete.
  2. A LOGGED FAILURE CARRYING A LONG TERM. Every failure 506 can record happens at the length gate, where the term is
     0 or 1 characters by definition -- a longer term either succeeds, and successful reads write no log row at all, or
     fails inside a caller-owned transaction, where error 3930 makes logging impossible (X1). So L4 asserts the term is
     absent from the log on the paths that DO log, and build/check_search_term_privacy.py is what covers the rest, by
     reading the module rather than running it. The two are not redundant and neither is sufficient alone.
  3. A BARE EXEC. @SearchTerm is REQUIRED, so a call with no arguments fails with error 201 before the body executes and
     nothing is logged. A0 asserts the error and A0b asserts the log is unchanged, rather than pretending otherwise.

A CLEAN RUN IS: 42 PASS, no FAIL, no CHANGED, no Msg line, and exactly one warning -- X1's. The cleanup line reports 10
retired rows rather than 11, because E1 was inserted already soft-deleted and the reset only touches visible rows.
Verified twice in a row, with identical output, on 2026-09-05.
***********************************************************************************************************************/

SET NOCOUNT ON;
SET XACT_ABORT OFF;   -- the refusal sections need the batch to continue after a caught error
GO

PRINT N'=== da4_searchhandlersource: dbo.uspSearchHandlerSource ===';
GO

-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
-- Reset. Retire anything a previous run left visible, under BOTH prefixes. Soft delete only.
-- ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
UPDATE dbo.HandlerSource
   SET IsDeleted             = 1
     , auditDeletedBy        = ORIGINAL_LOGIN ()
     , auditDeletedDateUtc   = SYSUTCDATETIME ()
     , auditModifiedBy       = ORIGINAL_LOGIN ()
     , auditModifiedDateUtc  = SYSUTCDATETIME ()
 WHERE (HandlerId LIKE N'MDPROBE0506%' OR HandlerId LIKE N'MD9990506%')
   AND IsDeleted = 0;
PRINT CONCAT (N'reset: retired ', @@ROWCOUNT, N' leftover fixture row(s).');
GO

